{%- comment -%}
  Drawer shell. Line items are rendered client-side by theme.js from /cart.js,
  but the initial state is server-rendered from the real cart so a slow or
  failed script never shows "your cart is empty" to someone mid-purchase.
{%- endcomment -%}
<div class="cart-drawer-scrim" data-cart-scrim></div>

<aside class="cart-drawer"
       data-cart-drawer
       role="dialog"
       aria-modal="true"
       aria-labelledby="cart-drawer-title"
       aria-hidden="true">

  <div class="cart-drawer-head">
    <h2 id="cart-drawer-title">Your cart</h2>
    <button type="button" class="hdr-icon" data-cart-close aria-label="Close cart">
      {% render 'icon', name: 'close' %}
    </button>
  </div>

  <div class="cart-drawer-body" data-cart-body>
    {%- if cart.item_count == 0 -%}
      <div class="cart-empty">
        <p class="cart-empty-title">Your cart is empty</p>
        <p class="cart-empty-note">Free shipping and cash on delivery on every order.</p>
        <a class="cta cart-empty-cta" href="{{ routes.all_products_collection_url }}">Start shopping</a>
      </div>
    {%- else -%}
      {%- for item in cart.items -%}
        <div class="cart-line" data-line-key="{{ item.key }}">
          <div class="cart-line-img">
            {%- comment -%}
              Through the shared snippet, like the cart page and every other
              image in the theme. Empty alt because the product title is right
              next to it — naming the image only makes a screen reader say the
              product twice. The presence check stays so .img-missing, which
              carries a 160px min-height for full-size plates, never lands in
              a 72px box.
            {%- endcomment -%}
            {%- if item.image -%}
              {% render 'responsive-image',
                 img: item.image,
                 alt: '',
                 widths: '72,144,216',
                 sizes: '72px',
                 base_width: 144,
                 loading: 'lazy' %}
            {%- endif -%}
          </div>
          <div>
            <p class="cart-line-title">{{ item.product.title }}</p>
            {%- unless item.variant.title == 'Default Title' -%}
              <p class="cart-line-variant">{{ item.variant.title }}</p>
            {%- endunless -%}
            {%- comment -%}
              The struck figure is the variant's compare_at_price — the MRP —
              and getting it here took solving the problem that used to rule it
              out. /cart.js carries no compare-at field, so the JS re-render
              could not know it, and a struck price that appeared on load and
              vanished on the first quantity change would be worse than none.

              It no longer vanishes: the seed below hands the script the
              compare-at for everything already in the cart, and theme.js
              fetches /products/{handle}.js once for anything added later. Both
              render paths read the same number, so they agree by construction —
              which is the property the old original_price compromise was
              bought to get, now had without giving up the real MRP.
            {%- endcomment -%}
            {%- render 'cart-line-mrp', item: item -%}
            <p class="micro">Qty {{ item.quantity }}</p>
          </div>
          <div class="cart-line-total">{{ item.final_line_price | money }}</div>
        </div>
      {%- endfor -%}
    {%- endif -%}
  </div>

  <div class="cart-drawer-foot" data-cart-foot {% if cart.item_count == 0 %}hidden{% endif %}>
    {%- comment -%}
      Label and value were the same size and weight, so "Subtotal" competed
      with the number a customer actually came here to read. The label is now
      secondary and the amount carries the weight.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- comment -%}
      A rejected quantity change used to reach the shopper only through the
      visually-hidden live region, so a sighted customer who stepped past the
      available stock saw the number go up, the totals stay put, and no reason
      given. Same element and same role as the buy box's error line, placed
      above the subtotal it explains.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <p class="cart-drawer-error" data-cart-error role="alert" hidden></p>

    {%- comment -%}
      Total Price summary.

      Every figure here is a field Shopify sent, or the difference between two
      of them. The theme calculates no prices — see the comment over money() in
      assets/theme.js — and this section does not change that.

        MRP Total     cart.original_total_price, the cart before any discount
        MRP Discount  original_total_price - total_price
        Cart Total    cart.total_price, after every discount Shopify applied
        Shipping      see below, the one figure that is not a cart field
        To Pay        cart.total_price

      The discount is taken as the difference of the two authoritative totals
      rather than read from cart.total_discount, so the column cannot fail to
      add up. Whatever Shopify discounted, by whatever mechanism — a code in
      the session, a line-level allocation, an automatic discount added later —
      MRP Total minus MRP Discount is Cart Total by construction, because the
      middle row is defined as the gap between the other two rather than
      sourced independently and hoped to agree.

      This row is also the answer to a subtotal that reads as wrong. A cart
      carrying the prepaid code came back 5% lower than the lines above it
      added up to, with nothing on screen accounting for the difference. The
      money was never missing; it was never labelled. It is labelled now.

      Shipping is the exception and is marked as such deliberately. /cart.js
      has no shipping field — Shopify does not rate shipping until checkout, so
      there is nothing authoritative to read this early. FREE is the store's
      standing promise, stated the same way in the empty-cart note above and in
      the buy box, not a rate quoted back from Shopify. If free shipping ever
      stops being universal, this line has to change with it; it cannot find
      out on its own.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- comment -%}
      MRP Total is the sum of each line's MRP times its quantity, not
      cart.original_total_price. They are different numbers and the difference
      is the whole point: original_total_price is the cart before Shopify's
      discounts, which for a product listed at 1,900 and sold at 1,299 is 1,299.
      It never sees the compare-at, so the breakdown reported an MRP identical
      to the price and a saving of nothing, on a product genuinely 601 off.

      Same rule as the line snippet, and it has to be the same rule or the
      column stops describing the rows above it: compare-at when it is really
      above what is charged, otherwise original_price, otherwise no markup at
      all and this line contributes exactly what the shopper pays.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- assign mrp_total = 0 -%}
    {%- for item in cart.items -%}
      {%- assign line_mrp = item.final_price -%}
      {%- if item.variant.compare_at_price > item.final_price -%}
        {%- assign line_mrp = item.variant.compare_at_price -%}
      {%- elsif item.original_price > item.final_price -%}
        {%- assign line_mrp = item.original_price -%}
      {%- endif -%}
      {%- assign line_mrp_total = line_mrp | times: item.quantity -%}
      {%- assign mrp_total = mrp_total | plus: line_mrp_total -%}
    {%- endfor -%}
    {%- if mrp_total < cart.total_price -%}{%- assign mrp_total = cart.total_price -%}{%- endif -%}
    {%- assign cart_saving = mrp_total | minus: cart.total_price -%}

    <div class="cart-summary">
      {%- comment -%}
        The panel precedes the bar in the DOM so it opens upward out of the bar,
        the way the reference does, without absolute positioning or a transform
        that would lift it out of the footer's flow. aria-controls is happy to
        point backwards; only the id has to match.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <div class="cart-summary-panel" id="cart-summary-panel" data-cart-summary-panel hidden>
        <dl class="cart-summary-rows">
          <div class="cart-summary-row">
            <dt>MRP Total</dt>
            <dd data-cart-mrp>{{ mrp_total | money }}</dd>
          </div>
          <div class="cart-summary-row" data-cart-discount-row {% if cart_saving <= 0 %}hidden{% endif %}>
            <dt>MRP Discount</dt>
            <dd class="cart-summary-cut" data-cart-discount>&minus;{{ cart_saving | money }}</dd>
          </div>
          <div class="cart-summary-row">
            <dt>Cart Total</dt>
            <dd data-cart-carttotal>{{ cart.total_price | money }}</dd>
          </div>
          <div class="cart-summary-row">
            <dt>Shipping</dt>
            <dd class="cart-summary-free">FREE</dd>
          </div>
          <div class="cart-summary-row cart-summary-row--pay">
            <dt>To Pay</dt>
            <dd data-cart-topay>{{ cart.total_price | money }}</dd>
          </div>
        </dl>
      </div>

      <button type="button" class="cart-summary-bar"
              data-cart-summary-toggle
              aria-expanded="false"
              aria-controls="cart-summary-panel">
        <span class="cart-summary-bar-l">
          <span class="cart-summary-bar-label">Total Price</span>
          <span class="cart-summary-bar-value" data-cart-subtotal>{{ cart.total_price | money }}</span>
        </span>
        <span class="cart-summary-bar-r">
          <span class="cart-summary-bar-save" data-cart-save-row {% if cart_saving <= 0 %}hidden{% endif %}>You Save <span data-cart-save>{{ cart_saving | money }}</span></span>
          <span class="cart-summary-chevron">{% render 'icon', name: 'chevron', size: 16 %}</span>
        </span>
      </button>
    </div>

    {%- comment -%}
      One primary action, and now only one. Checkout and View cart were once
      both full-width buttons of near-equal weight; View cart was demoted to a
      text link, and is gone entirely now. In a drawer that already lists every
      line, carries the stepper for each one and totals the order, a trip to a
      second page showing the same cart was a way out of the purchase wearing
      the costume of a way forward.

      There is no prepaid button here any more, and it must not come back.

      It was not merely a second CTA competing with Checkout. It navigated to
      /discount/CODE?redirect=/checkout, and that endpoint does not preview a
      saving — it writes the discount code onto the shopper's cart, where
      Shopify keeps it for the rest of the session. One tap and every later read
      of /cart.js comes back 5% lower, on this drawer, on the cart page and at
      checkout, whether or not the shopper ever pays online. A customer who
      tapped it and then chose cash on delivery still got the online-payment
      price, because nothing in the flow ever takes the code back off.

      That is the whole explanation for the subtotal reported as wrong: the
      figure was Shopify's real cart total for a cart that really did carry a
      discount code. The theme calculates no prices at all — see the comment
      over money() in assets/theme.js — so the only way that number could move
      was for something to change the cart, and this button was the something.

      The buy box keeps its prepaid offer, where the shopper is choosing how to
      pay and the trip to checkout is the point. Reaching the same endpoint from
      the cart drawer, after the decision to buy has already been made, only
      discounted orders that were going to be placed anyway.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <a class="cta cta-block" href="/checkout">Proceed to checkout</a>

    {%- comment -%}
      Three facts, and the separator is the theme's middot rather than a round
      bullet so this line sits in the same typographic family as the trust lines
      on the homepage and the closing buy block.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <p class="cart-drawer-note">Secure Payments &middot; Made in India &middot; Easy Returns</p>
  </div>

  {%- comment -%}
    Compare-at for everything already in the cart, handed to the script so it
    starts with what Liquid can see and /cart.js cannot.

    Without this the first AJAX repaint would drop every struck MRP until a
    products fetch came back — the precise flicker that made the previous
    implementation settle for original_price instead. theme.js still fetches
    /products/{handle}.js for anything added after load, so the seed is a head
    start rather than the only source, and a cart the seed does not cover
    degrades to no strikethrough rather than to a wrong one.

    Prices are in paise, the unit every Shopify money field uses, so nothing
    here needs converting on the way in.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <script type="application/json" data-cart-mrp-seed>
    {
      {%- for item in cart.items -%}
        "{{ item.variant_id }}": {{ item.variant.compare_at_price | default: 0 }}{%- unless forloop.last -%},{%- endunless -%}
      {%- endfor -%}
    }
  </script>
</aside>

{% schema %}
{ "name": "Cart drawer" }
{% endschema %}
